  "response": "Ran `crimes context src/billing.ts` against the fixture — 8 findings on the file (1 high, 5 medium, 2 low), plus two `contract_drift` findings elsewhere that name `src/billing.ts` as the counterparty.\n\n## Top 3 changes\n\n### 1. Break `generateInvoice` into pure helpers (`crime_00015`, God Function — severity 0.9, confidence 0.95)\n\n`billing.ts:50-253` is 204 lines, 3.4× the 60-line domain threshold, and its own comment enumerates eight responsibilities. This is the change with the best ratio because four other findings live *inside* it and fall out of the extraction:\n\n- **`crime_00012`** (Temporal Recklessness) — 5 of the 7 clock reads (L57, 63, 92, 238, 250) are in this body. Extracting `loyaltyDiscount(subtotal, daysSince)` and taking `now` as a parameter makes the discount tiers testable without freezing wall time.\n- **`crime_00040`** (Catch and Release, L245-247) — the `while (attempts < 3)` \"retry\" loop with a bare `catch {}` containing only a comment. Extracted into `sendInvoiceEmail()`, the swallow becomes a visible signature decision (throw, or return a typed result) instead of a thing buried at line 245 of 204.\n- **Duplicated tax policy** — the country→rate ladder at L145-154 is a byte-for-byte copy of `tax()` at L257-268. The scanner doesn't flag this pair directly, but the file's own comment admits it. Delete the inline copy and call `tax()`; today a VAT change has to land in two places with nothing linking them.\n- **`crime_00004`** (String Sprinkles, `\"enterprise\"`) — `user.plan === \"enterprise\"` at L75 is one of 4 scattered copies across `src/api/plan.ts`, `src/jobs/plan-sync.ts`, `src/ui/pricing.ts`. Pulling the plan multiplier into a `PLAN_DISCOUNT` map removes this file's copy.\n\nKeep `generateInvoice` as a thin orchestrator over `priceLines` / `loyaltyDiscount` / `applyPromo` / `scoreRisk` / `tax` / `currencyFor` / `renderEmail`.\n\n**Precondition:** `src/billing.test.ts` cannot catch a regression from this. `crime_00025` — the \"renders the enterprise plan\" test has **0 expect calls**; `crime_00031` — \"creates an invoice\" asserts `toBeTruthy()` against a hardcoded local stub that never touches `billing.ts`. There is no net under this refactor. Characterize the current pricing output first, then extract.\n\n### 2. Fix `calculateRefundPreview` — the name lies and the rule above it isn't implemented (`crime_00013` + `crime_00014`)\n\nThese two findings are five lines apart and are really one defect:\n\n```ts\n// Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves.   <- crime_00014, L39\nexport async function calculateRefundPreview(user, invoice): Promise<number> {\n  await saveRefundAudit(user.id, invoice.id);      // <- crime_00013\n  await sendRefundEmail(user.email, invoice.id);\n  return invoice.totalCents;\n}\n```\n\n`crime_00013` (False Identity, conf 0.80) catches the `calculate*Preview` prefix promising a pure read while the body writes an audit record and sends mail. `crime_00014` (Logic in the Alibi, conf 0.74) catches the authorization rule that exists only as prose — its evidence line \"domain terms not found nearby: owner, plan, billing, refund\" is literally true: there is no `user.role` check, no `plan` check, nothing. The comment describes a policy the function does not enforce.\n\nThe failure mode is concrete: an agent asked to \"show a refund preview on the invoice row\" reads the name, assumes it's pure, and maps it over a list — sending a refund email per row, to any caller, regardless of role. Fix: rename to `previewAndNotifyRefund` (or split the pure `invoice.totalCents` read from the two effects), and turn the comment into an executable guard that throws unless `user.role === \"owner\"` or support approval is present. Effort is small; this is the only item on the list that's arguably a live bug rather than a risk.\n\n### 3. Unify the `Invoice` / `User` declarations with `src/api/state.ts` (`crime_00033` severity **0.96**, `crime_00032` severity 0.90)\n\n`crime_00033` carries the highest severity in the entire 42-finding scan. `Invoice` is declared twice — `billing.ts:19` with 11 fields and `src/api/state.ts:9` with 2 — with 100% overlap on the smaller side and **6 disagreements, every one on a critical field**: `userId` (identifier), `subtotalCents` / `discountCents` / `taxCents` / `currency` (money), `createdAt` (timestamp). `User` splits the same way on `createdAt`. As the finding puts it: *nothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other* — `tsc` sees two unrelated interfaces and is happy.\n\nFix shape: export the types from `billing.ts` (or better, a `src/types.ts` once you're splitting the file per `crime_00037`, God File at 311/300 lines) and have the other side derive — `type Invoice = Pick<BillingInvoice, \"id\" | \"totalCents\">`. Then a new money field reaches both, or fails the build.\n\nOne caveat on ranking this third rather than first despite the severity: `src/api/state.ts` opens with \"This file is fixture material — do not import it from real code,\" so in this fixture the drift is inert. In a real repo with the same finding, it would move to #1.\n\n## Worth noting about the scan itself\n\n`crime_00012`'s evidence says *\"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading\"*, and downgrades the finding to medium on that basis. That's wrong for 2 of the 7: `Date.now()` at L92 feeds `daysSince`, which selects the 10%/5%/2% loyalty discount at L95-101 — the clock read decides money. Same shape at L273 → the `daysSince > 7/14/30` ladder in `recommendUpgrade`. The detector appears to only count a reading as branch-deciding when it appears directly in the comparison, not one variable-assignment hop away. Injecting the clock is a higher priority than the finding's own text suggests.\n\n## Skipped, deliberately\n\n- `crime_00021` (Commented-Out Corpse, L33-37) — already has a suppression in `.crimes/suppressions.json` with a stated reason and a removal target. Leave it; revisit at v0.3.\n- `crime_00042` (Unfinished Business, 4 markers) — the FIXME at L183 (PII in stdout) is real and should become a ticket, but the marker density itself isn't the problem.",
